libutils.a now has a new feature, with parameter lists. Here follows the
doc for parameter lists.

The idea is that every cado tool (so far polyselect/*, sieve/makefb, and
sieve/sieve, for an example) can have its working configuration either
completely within a file, or completely on the command line.

The old way still works unchanged. There are now just many more ways to
call programs, like:

polyselect/polyselect 71641520761751435455133616475667090434063332228247871795429

or with a config file:
polyselect/kleinjung << EOF
M=5e20
degree=5
l=8
n=3835722565249558322197842586047190634357074639921908543369929770877061053472916307274359341359614012333625966961056935597194003176666977
pb=256
EOF

The syntax of config files that can be read is the union of the
ggnfs/cado format, and the params file for the now obsolete cadofactor.pl
script:

file=(<comment-line> | <non-comment>)*
comment-line = <empty-line>|<white>#.*
non-comment = <white><key><white><separator><white><value><white>(#.*)?
white=<whitespace>*
key=<alphabetic>[<alphanumeric>_]*
separator=(:= | : | =)
value=<non-space>+

I see several benefits.
- for very quick tests, putting everything on the cmdline is handy.
- for regression tests, being able to put everything in a file is handy
- this deletes some code (but adds more). The cost per extra parameter is
  lower.


The processing goes through two steps:
- build a dictionary of (key, value) pair (as character strings).
- parse some values to set variables in the program.

###################################################################
# Quick documentation for the impatient.
#
# (note: for c++ code, go to the next section)

Common accepted syntaxes for options are --foo blah, -foo blah, or
foo=blah. On a first pass, keys are recognized as strings, and parsing
comes later on once lexing has completed.

Some terminology first.

``switches'' is the term I use for option which trigger something by their
mere presence, not requiring any other ``value'' input. The archetypal
example is for example --verbose.

``aliases'' is a means of indicating that a given option may be
referenced in more than one manner.

Effort has been put towards making it possible and easy to source a
config file hosting a (possibly large) number of options.



To use params.[ch]pp, your main() function must follow the following
pattern.

    cxx_param_list pl;

    /* documentation, if any. See below */
    /* switches, if any. See below */
    /* aliases, if any. See below */

    pl.process_command_line(argc, argv, false);
    /* If you want to do some other things with the arguments that haven't
     * been eaten at all, pass "true" as the last parameter above, then
     * act on the remaining argc and argv.

    /* Now parse the values corresponding to options, and map their
     * meaning somewhere */
    const char * tmp;

    /* p.lookup_old() gives a const char * pointer which will
     * live only for as long as the param_list structure isn't freed. The
     * return value is NULL if no such key exists. pl_has() works
     * similarly and returns an std::string const *. pl.lookup() returns
     * a fresh std::string. */

    if ((tmp = pl.lookup_old("subdir")) != NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "--subdir is no longer supported."
                " prepend --out with a path instead.\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    /* ... */

    /* It is possible to strdup() it of course */
    working_filename = strdup(pl.lookup_old("out"));
    if (working_filename == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Required argument --out is missing\n");
        exit(1);
    }
    /* ... */


    /* all parsing functions return 1 when the key was found, 0 if not.
     * If no key was found, no assignment is performed and the default
     * value set beforehand remains */
    pl.parse("hslices", split[0]);
    pl.parse("vslices", split[1]);

    int split[2] = {1,1};
    pl.parse("nbuckets", split);

    /* Good practice mandates that unused parameters trigger a warning,
     * if not an error. Only unused parameters from command line are
     * considered for issuance of a warning. Unused parameters found in
     * config files are considered normal and trigger nothing.
     */
    if (pl.warn_unused()) {
        usage();
    }


It is also possible to configure switches. Configure switches goes toghether
with specifying a pointer to the switch value, which must have type int.

    pl.configure_switch("--legacy");
    pl.configure_switch("--remove-input");
    pl.configure_switch("--pad);

    /* process argc and argv */

    pl.parse("--legacy", legacy);
    pl.parse("--remove-input", remove_input);
    pl.parse("--pad", pad);


Aliases:
    pl.configure_alias("--pad", "--square");
    // --> means that the switch --pad may be aliased as --square

    pl.configure_alias("out", "output-name");
    // means that --out, -out, out=, and so on, may be aliased as
    // --output-name, etc.

    There's one pitfall. If you use the former syntax for a non-switch
    argument, (e.g. --out -> --output-name), then the other forms will
    not be aliased.


###################################################################
# Best practices and convenience wrapper for c++ code

For c++ code, use the `cxx_param_list` type.

Parameter handling encompasses several distinct things.

 1. declaring the variable that will hold the parameter value. (e.g., an
    int, but many other types are possible.) Note that by design, this type
    must be default constructible
 2. specifying a default value.
 3. documenting the parameter.
 4. telling the parameter layer if it is a switch or not.
 5. actually parsing the parameter, and abort if a mandatory parameter is
    missing.
 6. perhaps do some consistency checking.

Example code that goes through these steps can be written as in [this
example](../tests/utils/test_params.cpp).



As an evolution of this, it makes sense that each part of the program
functionality is embedded in a class. In this model, the different steps
above are moved to a separate class, while the main processing such as
the `parse_command_line()` and `warn_unused()` calls remain within the
`main` function. We recommend that parsing takes place in the constructor
of the object, while the documentation / configuration steps can be done
in static methods. [This example](../tests/utils/test_params_struct.cpp)
illustrates this.
 - the main benefit is that the internal aspects of each parameter is
   moved away from `main()`. It also makes it possible to organize
   parameters in a more consistent manner.
 - we do have downsides, though. Some information is repeated over and
   over, such as each parameter type, the default values, or the key.
   Changing any of these implies 


The third evolution allows to get rid of most of the repetitions by
moving all information in the same place. The param layer defines three
structure templates, namely `parameter`, `parameter_mandatory`, and
`parameter_switch`. Each is automatically convertible to the target type
(but in some cases it makes sense to force this conversion). This is
exemplified in [this code](../tests/utils/test_params_declarator.cpp),
which as of this writing (Apr 2026) is the recommended way.



###################################################################
# Slightly longer doc. (not maintained up to date)

Roadmap for use:

** BUILDING THE DICTIONARY **

start with:

    cxx_param_list pl;

    argv++, argc--;
    for( ; argc ; ) {

then we're parsing arguments in argv,argc as we always have been. The
only difference (for some programs) is that now argv is shifted so that
the ``current'' argument is at argv[0]

the param_list routines are good at:
- parsing arguments of the form --degree 42 , or -d 42 , or degree=42.
- parsing config files.

So far, they are _not_ made for:
- booleans (-skip-something)
- switches (-verbose)
- things not foreseen.

Therefore, before giving your argv,argc to the param_list routines, you
have the occasion to parse your command line as you always have been.
Just do so only for the strict necessary, since for those arguments, you
won't get the benefit of allowing information to come from config files:

    if (strcmp(argv[0], "-v") == 0) { verbose++; argv++,argc--; continue; }

You may call the param_list member functions to recognize 
arguments on the command line, like:

    if (pl.update_cmdline(argc, argv)) { continue; }

This will accept just everything that ``resembles'' a parameter.
Formally, a parameter matches:
    (-<key> <value> | --<key> <value> | <key>=<value>)
    where <key>=<alphabetic>[<alphanumeric>_]*
Upon success, 1 is returned, and argv, argc advance by the right
number of positions.

If we haven't hit a 'continue' statement within the loop already, then we
might have some unparsed garbage on the command line (1). Or just
something special (2). Or we might be very polite and allow the user to
specify the name of an input parameter file, freeform, to be parsed right
now (3). Or we've got some very peculiar way of receiving config
arguments, so we'll catch them now (4).

To illustrate these cases, here are some snippets. For case (2):

      if (strspn(argv[0], "0123456789") == strlen(argv[0])) {
          pl.add_key("n", argv[0], cado::params::origin::FROM_CMDLINE);
          argv++,argc--;
          continue;
      }

The call to add_key above is almost an innard, and having it in the API
is just an ugly convenience measure. In particular, notice that it's your
job at this point to position argv,argc correctly for the next round.

After such a test, case (3) can be handled so:

      if ((f = fopen(argv[0], "r")) != NULL) {
          pl.read(f, false);
          fclose(f);
          argv++,argc--;
          continue;
      }

End of story, case (1) would go as such:

    pl.fail("Unhandled parameter {}\n", argv[0]);

The ``peculiar cases'' (case (4)) are to be handled in a way which
depends exactly on how parameters are expected.

Extra: parameters may have aliases. See cxx_param_list::configure_alias

** PARSING VALUES **

All the cxx_param_list::parse_* routines do parsing, and set variables. They
return 1 on success, 0 on failure.

It is possible to update the dictionary again depending on the result of
a parsing operation. For example, polyselect reads stdin only if it needs
it for knowing n:

  int have_n = pl.parse("n", poly->n);

  if (!have_n) {
      if (verbose) {
          fprintf(stderr, "Reading n from stdin\n");
      }
      pl.read(stdin, false);
      have_n = pl.parse("n", poly->n);
  }

The cxx_param_list::parse functions should be easily understood.

Eventually, it is possible to make sure that all command-line parameters
were used, and warn otherwise:

  if (pl.warn_unused()) {
      pl.fail("Not all arguments were parsed\n");
  }

###################################################################

There is (yet) some more documentation text in params.hpp

